course: dictionary
lesson: 3269
title: Erase: rub out, delete, forget
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it. Erase means to rub out writing, to delete something completely, or to remove a memory or feeling so it is gone.
explain: «The last moment with someone can feel huge, but one rude or careless moment does not erase a whole year.» The clue is «does not»: one moment cannot wipe out a whole year, so erase means take away completely, even something as big as a year of memories.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «one rude moment does not remove a whole year» still makes sense, so you have got it. At school you erase pencil marks with a rubber, and on a computer you erase files.
fact: Erase means to remove something completely.
fact: You can erase pencil or pen marks with a rubber, or wipe the whiteboard clean.
fact: On a computer, to erase means to delete a file, message or recording.
fact: In stories, people sometimes wish they could «erase a memory», so that it is forgotten.
fact: In the US the tool is called an «eraser»; in the UK it is called a «rubber».
exercise: «One rude or careless moment does not erase a whole year.» What does erase mean here? | *Remove or wipe away, so it is gone | Shine brightly | Make it longer | Write it down in a diary
exercise: Which tool do you use to erase pencil marks in a UK classroom? | *Rubber | Glue stick | Ruler | Pencil sharpener
exercise: Which computer action means the same as erase? | Print | *Delete | Save | Open
test: Someone says, «I wish I could erase that memory.» What do they want? | To feel hungry | *To make the memory go away and be forgotten | To write the memory in a book | To keep the memory forever
test: The teacher wants to erase the whiteboard. Which word means the same? | Copy | *Rub out | Paint | Hide
exam: Which sentence uses erase to mean forget a feeling? | *She tried hard to erase the sad feeling from her mind | They walked to the park after lunch | He drew a bright red picture | She counted the coins in her purse
